+++ b/Large-Response-and-Diagram-Handling.md
@@ -30,6 +30,8 @@ Splitting happens *between* lines. It cannot help when a **single statement** is
 - an over-long **message** matches no parse rule, so the parser abandons the whole diagram and the fragment renders as `Syntax Error?`, taking every other call in it down with it;
 - an over-long **coloured note bar** overflows the engine's own JavaScript stack (`RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`) and produces no SVG at all, only the error's text. The same stack runs out, since the 1.2026.8beta1 build, on a long **block opener** (on the 1.2026.6 build openers were a parse limit like messages), and on a long **component diagram edge label**, **participant name**, **activity action** or **swimlane**: the engine draws its `RangeError` picture, or `Syntax Error?` where the overflow happens while it chooses a command (an opener, a name or an action), in place of the whole diagram (capped since 4.14.5).
 
+**5.0.0's engine draws far past every stack limit here.** Its TeaVM, 0.17.0-dev-2, no longer walks a pattern once per character on the stack: in node it draws a `loop` label of 16,000 characters, a coloured bar of 6,000 and a participant's name of 8,000, and fails past those only where the diagram is wider than its 98,304-pixel limit; in a Chrome worker an earlier master on the same TeaVM drew an edge label of 102,400 characters and a participant's name of 12,800, in both its engines. It still runs out of stack on deep nesting (from about 2,000 groups inside one another in node), and fails that as text. The constants below stay where the 4.x engine's worker put them in 5.0.0 (see [[The Wasm engine|PlantUML-Browser-Rendering#the-wasm-engine-500]]).
+
 **The message limit is PlantUML's own; every other limit here belongs to the JavaScript build.** Measured both ways — real Java PlantUML via `Kronikol.PlantUml.Ikvm`, and by extension `PlantUmlRendering.Local`, refuses a 2,001-character message exactly as the JS build does, but draws a 2,000-character `loop` label and a 3,000-character coloured `hnote` without complaint. So the 2,000 cap is load-bearing whichever renderer you use, while every other cap is a concession to the TeaVM build that `BrowserJs` and `NodeJs` use — which is the default, and the one that produces a self-contained report anyone can open. Because the same generated source may be rendered either way, every cap is applied when the source is written rather than when a renderer is chosen.
 
 > **Measuring this is easy to get wrong.** When a message statement is too long the parser falls back to reading the source as a *class* diagram, and that fallback often succeeds — drawing classes named after the tokens, echoing the label text, and emitting no `Syntax Error?` banner at all. A check of the form "is the label in the SVG?" therefore passes for both outcomes. The signal that separates them is that a sequence diagram draws each participant **twice**, as a head box and a foot box.
